WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.755
Supply chain: models, packages, and MCP servers.

2
00:00:04.035 --> 00:00:05.016
Model weights.

3
00:00:05.296 --> 00:00:11.717
Many PyTorch checkpoints use Python’s pickle format, and loading a pickle can execute arbitrary code.

4
00:00:11.997 --> 00:00:19.933
Hugging Face scans uploaded pickles and flags suspicious imports, and its docs say plainly that the scan is not foolproof.

5
00:00:20.213 --> 00:00:31.563
Prefer the safetensors format, which stores tensors without executable content, load from publishers you trust, and pin a specific revision (a commit hash) rather than a moving branch.

6
00:00:31.843 --> 00:00:39.245
OWASP’s supply chain entry adds model provenance, license review, and red-teaming third-party models for your use case.

7
00:00:39.525 --> 00:00:40.485
Packages.

8
00:00:40.765 --> 00:00:47.635
Pin dependencies with a lockfile, scan them for known vulnerabilities in CI, and review new ones before adding them.

9
00:00:47.915 --> 00:00:54.549
AI tooling moves fast, and fast-moving packages are where typosquats and compromised releases land.

10
00:00:54.829 --> 00:00:56.237
MCP servers.

11
00:00:56.517 --> 00:01:01.531
A local MCP server is a program running on your machine with your privileges.

12
00:01:01.811 --> 00:01:19.581
The MCP specification’s security guidance lists the consequences: arbitrary code execution, data exfiltration, and data loss from a malicious or buggy server, and requires clients to show the exact startup command and get explicit consent before running a new one.

13
00:01:19.861 --> 00:01:34.624
It recommends sandboxing servers with minimal default privileges, forbids token passthrough (a server must not accept tokens that weren’t issued to it), and recommends progressive, least-privilege scopes rather than requesting everything up front.

14
00:01:34.904 --> 00:01:43.544
Treat adding an MCP server like adding a dependency with shell access: review it, pin it, sandbox it, and scope its credentials.

15
00:01:43.824 --> 00:01:44.976
Hosted models.

16
00:01:45.256 --> 00:01:47.197
Your provider is a supplier too.

17
00:01:47.477 --> 00:01:58.187
Models are deprecated and behavior changes between versions, so pin model ids, read deprecation notices, and rerun your evals and red-team set before switching.
